<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">County Mayo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">County Mayo is a county in the province of Connacht, in the west of Ireland, with Castlebar as its county town. It includes Croagh Patrick, the pilgrimage mountain, and Achill Island. Ruled in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> by the Lower Mac William <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-burgh">Burkes</a> and the O'Malleys of Clew Bay, it was shired in 1585, and in 1798 a French force landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/killala">Killala</a> on its coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/county-mayo.jpg" alt="County Mayo" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kiran Madhusudhanan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beautiful_Achill_Islands_in_County_Mayo_of_Ireland.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Inspired by the French Revolution, the Society of United Irishmen led a rebellion in 1798, supported by a small French landing in <b>County Mayo</b>; it was suppressed with heavy loss of life.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The rebellion of the Society of United Irishmen in 1798, in which many thousands died and a French force landed in <b>County Mayo</b>, convinced William Pitt the Younger that union was needed to secure Ireland during the war with revolutionary France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Auteini, or Autini, are listed in Ptolemy's Geography of about 150 on the west coast of Ireland, between the Nagnatae to the north and the Gangani (Ireland) to the south, roughly in modern Galway or <b>Mayo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/auteini">Auteini</a></li><li><blockquote>His descendants ruled much of modern <b>Mayo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/mac-william-iochtar">Mac William Iochtar</a></li><li><blockquote>Ptolemy's Geography of about 150 places the Nagnatae on the north-west coast of Ireland, roughly in Sligo or north <b>Mayo</b>, and lists a settlement called Nagnata, which he describes as a notable town.</blockquote><a href="/state/nagnatae">Nagnatae</a></li></ul></section>
